{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>All your males shall be seen.</b> And not the females, for females are exempt from the appearance (at the pilgrimage festivals), and likewise the blind and the lame and those like them. And the reason for this in its hidden wisdom (sodo) is that the Glory was seen in the cloud before the eyes of all Israel, and that cloud was exceedingly pure and bright like the appearance of glass, in which everyone who looks at it sees his own form reflected from within it, while what was inside the cloud was not visible from without; and since it is not fitting to the honor of the Shekhinah that these (reflections) should be seen from within her, therefore they (women) were exempt from the appearance. And this is what it says: 'when you go up to be seen before the face of YHWH your Elohim.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: one who is blind in one eye is exempt from the appearance, for it says יראה כל זכורך — 'יראה יראה' (shall be seen … shall be seen): just as he comes to see, so he comes to be seen; just as seeing is with two eyes, so being seen is with two eyes. And likewise you find that they called Abraham by these two expressions, as it says (Genesis 22:14) 'YHWH יראה — on the mount of YHWH יראה.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The verse partially cited as 'בעלותך לראות את פני ה\\' אלהיך' echoes Exodus 23:17 / 34:23; the midrashic reading of יראה יראה as 'see/be seen' derives from Hagigah 8a, here rendered as the Hebrew idiom requires; רז\"ל resolved as רבותינו זכרונם לברכה."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:03.437237777Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}